  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 days (Through Monday)...\n\nHeights build a bit over the region today as the ridge axis over \nthe central US pivots slightly. Temperatures will be near to a few\ndegrees warmer than yesterday, with highs in the upper 90s to\n100s, warmest over the east. Ridging will gradually shift east\nthrough mid week, allowing temperatures to cool over the west on \nWednesday, with hot temperatures persisting over the east. Very \nwarm overnight lows tonight and high temperatures into the 95 to \n105 degree range can be expected over eastern Montana and Sheridan\nCounty, Wy for Wednesday. The Extreme Heat Warning continues \ntoday for areas across southeast Montana, including Billings, and \nthen extends into Wednesday for the southeast corner of the state.\n\nMonsoonal moisture begins surging into the region in earnest from\nthe southwest today. Easterly low level flow will aid in moisture\ntransport as well over the lower elevations. This will cause\nunusually high PWATs for this time of year (200% or more of \nnormal) to persist through at least mid week. Dewpoints in the \nupper 50s and lower 60s may be common with a relatively humid air \nmass and temps in the lower to mid 90s. Scattered thunderstorms \ncan be expected to move over western and central areas later this \nafternoon and evening, and yet again on Wednesday, when PWATs are \nat their highest. Thunderstorms could be strong, with gusty winds \nand heavy rainfall, with hail to a lesser extent. SPC has a \nmarginal risk for severe thunderstorms over central and western \nareas with modest instability and shear in place. We have gone \nahead and issued a Flash Flood Watch for areas along and north of\nI-90 from Livingston to Billings for late this afternoon and \nevening. Currently, portions of central Montana are mentioned in \nthe excessive rainfall outlook through mid week, and additional \nflash flood watches may be needed.\n\nMonsoonal flow looks to continue to transport anomalous moisture \ninto the region for the remainder of the week, with weak impulses \nprogged to track through the area at times. This will bring \nmoderate chances for daily showers/thunderstorms to the area, \ngreatest over the mountains and into western and central areas.\nTemperatures will range from the upper 80s to 90s most days.\nHeading into early next week, ensembles hint that the flow aloft \ncould shift to the northwest, which would bring a cool down and \nmore widespread temperatures in the 80s. STP",
